{"name":"Candle Wax","hex":"#f2eacf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"candle wax","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any material, such as stearin or tallow, used to make the body of candles"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/candle_wax"}],"entries":[{"title":"Candle","blocks":[{"text":"A candle is an ignitable wick embedded in wax, or another flammable solid substance such as tallow, that provides light, and in some cases, a fragrance. A candle can also provide heat or a method of keeping time. Candles have been used for over two millennia around the world, and were a significant form of indoor lighting until the invention of other types of light sources. Although electric light has largely made candle use nonessential for illumination, candles are still commonly used for functional, symbolic and aesthetic purposes and in specific cultural and religious settings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg/330px-LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle","wikidata":"Q12888135"},{"title":"Wax","blocks":[{"text":"Waxes are a diverse class of organic compounds that are lipophilic solids that are malleable near ambient temperatures. They include higher alkanes and lipids, typically with melting points above about 40 °C (104 °F), melting to give low viscosity liquids. Waxes are insoluble in water but soluble in nonpolar organic solvents such as hexane, benzene and chloroform. Natural waxes of various types are produced by plants and animals and occur in petroleum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Beeswax_foundation.jpg/330px-Beeswax_foundation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wax","wikidata":"Q124695"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o